<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dar Fertit</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dar Fertit, the land of the Fertit, was the name given in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> to the country south of the sultanate, in what is now western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> and the north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>. Its varied peoples, called Fertit, were raided for slaves by the sultans of Darfur and later by traders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley, and the captives were sent north along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darb-el-arbain">Darb el-Arba'in</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dar Fertit, also spelled Dar Fartit, lay in the lowlands south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>, watered by tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>, in parts of what are now south-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">western Sudan</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>. The terrain is inhospitable and becomes impassable in the wet season. Many ethnic groups and languages have long lived there, some since before 1800 and others arriving later, and the region never formed a single polity. Today it consists mostly of the western part of Raga County in Western Bahr el Ghazal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until the 1840s the region, like the rest of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>, was claimed by no state, while Muslim sultanates with slave-based economies, among them Dar Fur, Dar Runga, Waddai and Dar al-Kuti, lay to the north and west. From the 1700s Dar Fur and other sultanates raided it for slaves or levied slaves from its communities. The name Fertit, whose origin is lost, came to mean the non-Muslims living south of Dar Fur, people whom the law of the sultanates held to be enslavable. Throughout the 1800s people from the west and north also fled into Dar Fertit to escape slave raids.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, then a domain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, pushed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> and westwards and annexed the region in 1873. It granted private merchants concessions to gather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves, worked from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">fortified camps</a> called zaribas. In the mid-1800s one of these merchant warlords, al-Zubayr, conquered Dar Fertit and made it his own domain; his zariba, Deim Zubeir, became the core of the modern town of that name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fertit is now a collective name for the groups of Western Bahr el Ghazal who are neither Dinka, Arab, Luo nor Fur. They speak different languages and have a history of fighting one another, but have drawn closer over time, largely in opposition to the Dinka. Fertit also live in the north-west of Western Equatoria and the far north-west of Northern Bahr el Ghazal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dār Fertit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultanate's economy rested on settled Fur agriculture in the Marrah highlands and surrounding plains, long-distance trade along the Darb al-Arba'in, and systematic slave raiding into the lands to the south, known as <b>Dar Fertit</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
